using static StrataLint.Scribe.DefinitionDsl;

namespace StrataLint.Scribe.Blueprint.D5.S3.Geometry.AlgebraicBoundary;

internal sealed class InteriorCubicFactorDocument : IScribeDocumentDefinition
{
    public DocumentDefinition Create() => DocumentDefinition.Create(ScribeNode.Create(
        "Cubic entire-boundary equations on compact convex bodies.",
        H("An interior zero gives an actual removable factor"),
        Blocks(
            Paragraph(Text("Let B be a compact convex subset of a finite real coordinate space, let a be an interior point, and let p be a nonzero real multivariate polynomial of total degree at most three. Suppose p vanishes at every point of the entire boundary and also at a. Then the actual Frechet derivative L=Dp(a) is nonzero. There is a nonzero polynomial g such that p(x)=L(x-a)g(x) for every x, with deg(g)+1=deg(p), and g vanishes on the entire boundary.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For every nonzero vector v, the translated convex gauge constructs two actual boundary points a+alpha v and a+beta v with alpha<0<beta. The polynomial obtained by substituting a+tv has degree at most deg(p), and its linear coefficient is L(v). When L(v)=0, its zero constant and linear coefficients give a double zero at t=0. The two distinct nonzero boundary roots then force the restriction to vanish identically. The zero direction is included separately. Thus p vanishes on the entire affine hyperplane L(x-a)=0. If L were zero, p would vanish everywhere, contrary to polynomial function extensionality and p being nonzero.")),
            Paragraph(Text("The linear polynomial l representing L(x-a) is constructed in the same coordinates. Choose v with L(v)=1 and substitute x-l(x)v into p. This polynomial vanishes everywhere because the substituted points lie on the actual affine hyperplane, so it is the zero polynomial. In the quotient by the ideal generated by l, this substitution fixes every coordinate. Therefore p belongs to that principal ideal, giving a genuine polynomial factorization p=lg. Polynomial total degree is additive for these nonzero factors, and l has degree one. The entire-boundary factor deletion theorem then applies to the continuous polynomial g.")),
            Paragraph(Text("From any nonzero entire-boundary equation of degree at most three, choose a nonzero equation of minimum total degree among all entire-boundary equations. The factor theorem excludes every interior zero of that minimum equation. If the body has nonempty interior, continuity and convex connectedness show that its sign is constant there; choose its sign once so it is strictly positive everywhere in the interior.")),
            Paragraph(Text("The original-body statement retains an arbitrary real normed carrier E, an arbitrary continuous linear coordinate equivalence e from E to the finite coordinate space, and the original compact convex set B in E. The polynomial at an original point x is evaluated at e(x). The coordinate homeomorphism transports the entire frontier and interior, and minimum degree is compared against every polynomial vanishing on the original entire frontier. No local boundary patch, smoothness, irreducibility, supplied factorization or supplied minimum equation is used.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For a normalization u(b)=1 and mutually inverse kernel coordinates J and A, affine substitution is represented by an actual polynomial with total degree no greater than the original. Homogenization is the sum over k of u raised to D-k times the k-th homogeneous component evaluated on the linear projection A, where D is the affine total degree. It is homogeneous of degree D and has its exact section value on b+J(v). A nonzero affine polynomial therefore gives a nonzero homogenization of exactly the same total degree. A nonzero homogeneous polynomial has nonzero restriction: normalization covers u(x) nonzero, and polynomial continuation along x+t b covers u(x)=0. This argument includes the zero-dimensional affine chart. For a positive-dimensional compact base with nonempty boundary, every nonzero boundary equation has positive degree. Its homogenization vanishes on the entire cone boundary, including the vertex. Consequently a homogeneous minimum cone equation restricts to a nonzero minimum affine equation of exactly the same total degree. Principality is a separate assertion.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For an arbitrary original real normed carrier E with continuous linear coordinates c, a closed salient cone C, and a homogeneous entire-boundary polynomial p of degree d at most three, fix any original interior direction e with p(c(e)) nonzero. The actual restriction is q_x(t)=p(c(te-x)). Its top coefficient is exactly p(c(e)), so its degree is exactly d for every original x. Off the axis, the two actual closed-ray endpoints supply two distinct real roots; polynomial division by one linear factor leaves degree at most two with the second root, which gives complete splitting into real linear factors. On the axis x=se, the exact polynomial identity is q_x=p(c(e))(X-s)^d, including x=0 and every repeated root. Splitting retains multiplicities; it is stronger than merely listing two real roots.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For a homogeneous minimum entire-boundary equation, absence of interior zeros follows from the actual compact base. The strictly positive normalization, its normalized interior point and the kernel coordinate equivalence are constructed from the original closed salient full cone. Exact degree and minimum correspondence transfer the minimum equation to the compact body, where an interior zero would yield a lower-degree entire-boundary equation. In the zero-dimensional kernel case every original vector is a scalar multiple of the normalized point, and nonzero homogeneity excludes an interior zero directly. Continuity on the convex original interior then supplies one common sign. The analytic branch argument uses these derived facts. For a point x in C and t<0, x+(-t)e lies in the original interior, so homogeneity excludes q_x(t)=0. Conversely, if x is outside C, the negative-cone ray endpoint gives an actual negative root; on the axis, the root s itself is negative. This yields equality between C and the entire set of points whose q_x roots are all nonnegative. Every interior direction and every original vector are retained. The original interior is exactly the connected component of the polynomial nonzero set containing the direction, and its closure is the original closed cone. Any other nonzero entire-boundary equation of the same minimum total degree is homogeneous: every lower homogeneous component vanishes on the entire frontier and would contradict minimum degree. Subtract a scalar multiple of the selected minimum equation to cancel its value at an interior point. If the difference were nonzero it would again be a minimum equation with an interior zero. Hence the original minimum equation is unique up to a nonzero real scalar. This argument concerns minimum degree at most three. It does not prove that the minimum equation divides every boundary equation of arbitrary degree.")),
            Paragraph(Text("Starting instead with an arbitrary nonzero, possibly nonhomogeneous polynomial vanishing on the entire boundary of the original closed salient cone in a nonzero real carrier, one can extract a nonzero homogeneous component q of positive degree d no greater than the original total degree, and q still vanishes on the entire boundary. Positive dilations are actual cone homeomorphisms and preserve the entire frontier. For each original boundary point y, the radial polynomial with coefficient k equal to the value of the k-th homogeneous component at c(y) vanishes for every positive scalar. It has infinitely many roots and is therefore zero, so each component vanishes at y. The sum of the components is the original nonzero polynomial, hence one component is nonzero. Finally the vertex belongs to the entire frontier: if zero were interior, positive rescaling would put every vector into the cone, contradicting salience. Vanishing at this same vertex excludes degree zero for the selected component. Selecting minimum degree first and then extracting a nonzero component gives an actual homogeneous minimum equation. Such a minimum equation is squarefree: if a squared nonunit factor divided it, deleting one copy would preserve vanishing at every boundary point and strictly reduce total degree. This establishes actual homogeneous squarefree minimum equations. General principality, and the finite-degree semialgebraic boundary prerequisite used to prove it in the source, are separate obligations.")),
            Paragraph(Text("In a one-dimensional original carrier, the constructed normalization u and normalized interior point b satisfy x=u(x)b for every vector. The cone is exactly u(x) nonnegative and its entire frontier is the original vertex. The polynomial representing u is homogeneous of total degree one. A polynomial vanishes on that entire frontier exactly when this linear polynomial divides it, by the actual hyperplane factorization. Thus the ray case has its actual principal boundary ideal, rather than a boundary-covering surrogate.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For the converse, suppose a homogeneous polynomial is nonzero at an original interior direction and its whole closed nonnegative-root branch is exactly the original cone. At a boundary point where the polynomial were nonzero, the actual line polynomial would have no zero root. Every real root is positive because the point belongs to the branch. Its finite set of roots has a strictly positive common lower bound epsilon, including the case of an empty root set. Translating the point backwards by epsilon times the direction shifts every real root by minus epsilon and therefore stays in the same whole branch. Adding that positive multiple of the interior direction then puts the original boundary point in the interior, a contradiction. Consequently this same polynomial vanishes on the entire original boundary. This uses the genuine whole-branch equality; hyperbolicity plus boundary covering alone does not supply it.")))));
}
